<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paris Peace Treaties (1947)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Peace Treaties were signed on 10 February 1947 between the Allied powers and the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> allies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>. They set new borders, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> and limits on armed forces. Italy lost its colonies, ceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodecanese">Dodecanese</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and lands on the Adriatic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a> became a free territory.</p></div>
